1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,425
The brush clears a stone fort, with crenellated walls and a single gate.

2
00:00:05,425 --> 00:00:11,819
From about the eighth century, a number of small kingdoms in northern and western India built forts like this.

3
00:00:11,819 --> 00:00:17,989
They are often called the Rajput kingdoms. Why did hill forts become the heart of a kingdom?

4
00:00:35,383 --> 00:00:42,449
How do we know? Inscriptions and grants record the kings. Chronicles and poems praise them.

5
00:00:42,449 --> 00:00:49,869
The Rajatarangini, a history of Kashmir, was written by Kalhana. Forts and temples still stand.

6
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:09,684
After the Pratiharas, north India split into many kingdoms.

7
00:01:09,684 --> 00:01:18,073
These are known as the Rajput kingdoms, though the term itself arose gradually. The Chahamanas, or Chauhans, ruled from Ajmer.

8
00:01:18,073 --> 00:01:24,715
Their best-known king was Prithviraj the Third. The Paramaras of Malwa had Dhar as their capital.

9
00:01:24,715 --> 00:01:27,451
Their ruler Bhoja was a patron of learning.

10
00:01:27,451 --> 00:01:33,544
The Chalukyas of Gujarat, also called Solankis, built the Dilwara temples at Mount Abu.

11
00:01:33,544 --> 00:01:43,602
The Chandelas of Bundelkhand built the temples of Khajuraho between about 950 and 1050 CE. The Gahadavalas held Kannauj.

12
00:01:43,602 --> 00:01:50,125
These kingdoms were often at war with each other, and they were often ruled by clan chiefs with strong ties of kinship.

13
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:54,931
The forts of Chittor, Gwalior and Ranthambore remind us of this age.

14
00:02:10,817 --> 00:02:14,677
Scholars disagree. Where did the Rajputs come from?

15
00:02:14,677 --> 00:02:19,636
One view holds that they descended from older Kshatriya lines of the Ganga plains.

16
00:02:19,636 --> 00:02:27,440
Another view holds that some clans grew out of groups that entered India, such as the Hunas and Gurjaras, and took Kshatriya rank.

17
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:35,557
A medieval tradition, the Agnikula legend, gives a third account, in which four clans arose from a sacrificial fire.

18
00:02:51,450 --> 00:03:00,227
Carve this tip into memory. Chauhan Ajmer, Paramara Dhar. Chandela Khajuraho. Three clans, three towns.

19
00:03:00,227 --> 00:03:03,595
The Chalukyas and Gahadavalas follow.

20
00:03:19,483 --> 00:03:35,801
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which Rajput clan ruled from Ajmer? The answer: the Chahamanas. Number two.

21
00:03:35,801 --> 00:03:51,805
Which clan built the temples of Khajuraho? The answer: the Chandelas. Number three. Who wrote the Rajatarangini?

22
00:03:51,805 --> 00:04:02,120
The answer: Kalhana. Now one line for Mains. Discuss the origin and political features of the Rajput kingdoms.

23
00:04:22,017 --> 00:04:31,824
The fort goes into its case. It stands for the clans of north and west India. Three facts to take away. A fort of the Rajput age.

24
00:04:31,824 --> 00:04:41,910
Chahamanas, Paramaras, Chandelas. Strong forts, strong clan ties. Next gallery: Arrivals from the West.

